-- title:
--   Branch primes with distinct tangent directions contract to distinct stalk primes
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q \ge 5$, an integer $M' \neq 0$ with $q \nmid M'$, and a prime $\ell \ge 3$ with $\ell \neq q$ and $\ell \nmid M'$; let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero that is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of order $q\ell$, with $\zeta$ a primitive $q$-th and $\xi$ a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity in $L$. Let $K$ be the intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ obtained as `laurentBaseChange` of the $X_H$ function field at level $(q\ell)^2 M'$ for $H = \ker\big((\mathbb{Z}/(q\ell)^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q\ell)^\times\big)$, i.e. the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image of that field. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, with $q$ in its maximal ideal, $\zeta$ in the image of $A$, acting on $K$ compatibly with $L$, and let $\varpi$ generate its maximal ideal. Let $j \in K$ be nonzero with image the base-changed $q$-expansion `jq` of the modular $j$-function. On the two-chart integral model $X =$ `TwoChartIntegralModel A K j` (the pushout of the spectra of the $A$-algebras of elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$, resp. $A[j^{-1}]$, along the middle chart), let $z$ be a point at which the image $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$, pulled back along `toBase` and taken as a germ, lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk, and let $y$ be a point of $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the $j$-finite chart algebra mapping to $z$ such that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the $j$-finite chart algebra to $\Omega$ with kernel the prime of $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in `ssJSet q Ω`, the set of $j \in \Omega$ such that every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $j$ has trivial $q$-torsion. Assume further given a complete discrete valuation domain $W$, a ring homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with maximal ideal generated by $\sigma\varpi$, power series $f, u, v \in W[[X_0, X_1]]$ with $u, v$ units and $f \equiv X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0, X_1)^{q+2}$, and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{X,z}$ onto $S = W[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma\varpi)v - fu)$. Writing $\mathrm{toC}$ for the map from the stalk to its completion and $\mathrm{mkS}$ for the quotient map onto $S$, the conclusion is: for all integers $a_1, b_1, a_2, b_2$ and all prime ideals $P_1, P_2$ of $S$ such that each $P_i$ omits at least one of the images of $X_0, X_1$, contains the image of $C(\sigma\varpi)$, and contains the image of $a_iX_0 + b_iX_1 + h_i$ for some $h_i \in (X_0, X_1)^2$, and such that $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$, the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ along $\mathrm{toC}$ followed by $e$ are distinct ideals of $\mathcal{O}_{X,z}$. (A local abbreviation for the germ homomorphism from the $j$-finite chart algebra to the stalk at $z$ is introduced; the conclusion does not refer to it.)
--
--   This is the statement that, at a supersingular point of the integral model of the modular curve in question, the branch primes of the completed local ring with distinct tangent directions modulo $q$ remain distinct after contraction to the (non-complete) stalk — the local picture of the Drinfeld/Katz–Mazur crossing of Igusa-type components. It is phrased relative to an arbitrary chart witness $(W, \sigma, f, u, v, e)$ for the Drinfeld form, and is used by the results producing the Drinfeld chart isomorphism at such points and the description of the inertia action on it.
